# Nobody complies: intention-to-treat versus the effect of treatment
next: {"title":"Instrumental variables and the LATE theorem","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/instrumental-variables-and-the-late-theorem.cast"}

## Winning Is Not Enrolling
voice: male
level: advanced
use: icon_array
with:
    total: 100
    groups:
      - {label: 'Enrolled: D=1', count: 25, color: supply}
    rest_label: Won, never enrolled
    title: 100 Oregon lottery winners

A: Across these four parts we separate two numbers that policy debates confuse constantly: the effect of being offered insurance, and the effect of having it — and Oregon is where the gap opens.
    draw title

A: Here are a hundred of the roughly thirty thousand people whose names came out of the drum in 2008 — every one of them has Z_i = 1, an offer to apply for Medicaid.
    draw rest group_0

B: And the blue ones — those are the people who actually ended up covered?
    point at.ref group_0 gesture circle

A: About a quarter of winners: D_i = 1. The rest never returned the paperwork in time, moved, or turned out ineligible — offer in hand, no coverage.
    draw legend_0 legend_rest

    pause 0.4

B: So the lottery randomized the letter, not the insurance.
    highlight rest effect glow

A: Precisely — Z_i is random by construction, and D_i follows it for only one person in four; that shortfall is not a flaw in the design, it is how ninety thousand applicants behave.
    highlight group_0 effect glow

    clear

A: Now the one comparison the experiment licenses: everybody drawn against everybody not drawn, taken as they were assigned.
    text t_win "Everyone drawn — Z_i = 1" x 250 y 610 font_size 30
    text t_lose "Everyone not drawn — Z_i = 0" x 750 y 610 font_size 30
    arrow arrow_diff t_lose -> t_win
    label lab_diff "difference in average outcome Y" below attach_to arrow_diff

A: That difference in average outcomes is the intention-to-treat effect.
    text t_itt "ITT = E[Y_i | Z_i = 1] − E[Y_i | Z_i = 0]" x 500 y 440 font_size 36

B: But three quarters of that 'treated' side were never treated — so the number must be wrong.
    point at.ref t_win gesture tap

A: Not wrong — diluted. It is an honest causal effect, averaged over a group where most people never took up the thing we care about.
    text t_note1 "Unbiased — but only 1 in 4 on the Z=1 side ever had D_i = 1" x 500 y 345 font_size 26

A: And that mixture is itself a policy quantity: a state budgeting for an expansion gets exactly this, take-up shortfall included.
    text t_note2 "The effect of the offer — what mailing 30,000 letters actually buys" x 500 y 285 font_size 26

B: Oh — so a headline reading 'Medicaid barely moved health' could be quoting an effect that is three-quarters made of people without Medicaid.
    highlight t_note1 effect pulse

A: That misreading is the standing danger — Oregon is the only randomized insurance experiment the United States has run since RAND, so its numbers get quoted for decades.
    text t_punch "ITT ≠ effect of coverage" #b5482e x 500 y 180 font_size 32
    mark mark_punch target t_punch kind box

A: Winning is not enrolling: the winners-versus-losers contrast is clean and causal — about the offer, diluted by everyone who never showed up.
    delivery grave

## Scaling up the dilution
voice: male
level: advanced
use: equation_steps
with:
    title: Scaling up the dilution
    steps:
      - {tex: '\text{ITT} = E[Y_i \mid Z_i = 1] - E[Y_i \mid Z_i = 0] = 0.05', note: 'Winners minus losers, everyone counted'}
      - {tex: 'E[D_i \mid Z_i = 1] - E[D_i \mid Z_i = 0] = 0.25', note: Only a quarter were moved onto coverage}
      - {tex: '0.05 = 0.25 \times \tau_{\text{moved}}', note: 'The dilution, written down'}
      - {tex: '\tau_{\text{moved}} = \dfrac{0.05}{0.25} = 0.20', note: Divide the dilution out}

    mark box_answer target step_3 kind box hidden true

    draw title

A: Now that we've seen the trap in comparing enrollees with non-enrollees, we keep the randomization untouched and repair the dilution by arithmetic — starting from the honest winners-minus-losers difference; say it comes out at five percentage points more people with a regular source of care.
    draw step_0 note_0

B: But those five points are spread over thirty thousand winners, three quarters of whom never got coverage at all.
    point at.ref step_0 gesture underline

A: Exactly — and that dilution is itself measurable: winning the lottery raised the share actually covered by about twenty-five points, not a hundred.
    draw step_1 note_1

A: And it's a difference in coverage rates, winners minus losers — a few losers found coverage by other routes, so they belong in this denominator too.
    highlight step_1 effect glow

    pause 0.3

A: So the intention-to-treat effect is a weighted average: three quarters of the winners were untouched and contribute nothing, and five points equals a quarter times whatever coverage did to the people it reached.
    draw step_2 note_2

B: Then dividing by a quarter gives the effect of insurance on everyone in the lottery — four times bigger, twenty points across the whole pool?
    point at.ref step_2 gesture tap

A: The arithmetic is right — twenty points — but the population is not everyone: it is only the people whose coverage the lottery actually changed.
    draw step_3 note_3 box_answer

B: Oh — so the number gets bigger, and at the same time the group it describes gets narrower.
    point at.ref box_answer gesture circle

A: And the division only earns its keep if the untouched three quarters really contributed zero — that winning a lottery you never used did nothing on its own; what licenses that step is next lecture's business.
    highlight step_2 effect pulse

    pause 0.4

B: Then why do the regulators reading trial reports insist on the diluted number instead of this rescaled one?
    clear keep ["title"]

A: Medicine met this problem first: Austin Bradford Hill's 1948 streptomycin trial for tuberculosis made randomized allocation the standard, and it immediately produced patients who stopped taking their pills.
    portrait hill of "Austin Bradford Hill" cameo true

A: The regulatory answer, written into the international trial guidelines, is intention-to-treat: analyse every patient as randomized, whatever they actually swallowed — because a drug that people abandon is a drug that does not work.
    erase hill

B: So the diluted number is the trustworthy one, and our division buys reach at the price of an assumption.
    show step_0 note_0 step_1 note_1 step_2 note_2 step_3 note_3 box_answer

A: That is the whole repair: intention-to-treat divided by the compliance it produced — a bigger effect about a smaller group of people.
    highlight step_3 effect glow delivery grave

## Which Number Does the Policy Question Want?
voice: male
level: advanced
use: decision_tree
with:
    root:
      id: q
      type: decision
      label: The policy question
      children: [{label: Run the offer again?, node: {id: offer, type: terminal, label: 'ITT: +4 pp'}}, {label: What does the program cost?, node: {id: budget, type: terminal, label: ITT again}}, {label: What does coverage do to a person?, node: {id: person, type: terminal, label: +4 ÷ 0.25 = +16 pp}}]

A: Now that we have seen how dividing by the take-up rate scales the diluted effect back up, one question is left: which of the two numbers do you actually report?
    draw node_q label_q

B: Surely the rescaled one — the diluted number is just the damaged version of the truth, isn't it?
    point at.ref node_q gesture tap

A: That is the trap of this whole lecture. Let's send three real questions down from this box.

A: First: should Oregon run this lottery again? Carry over our running numbers — an intention-to-treat effect of four points across everyone whose name was drawn.
    draw edge_q_offer branchlabel_q_offer node_offer label_offer

    pause 0.3

B: But only about a quarter of them ever enrolled, so those four points are watered down by the three who did not.
    point at.ref label_offer gesture circle

A: Watered down, and exactly right — the state can mail an invitation, it cannot make anyone return the form, so four points per name drawn is what the offer delivers.
    highlight node_offer label_offer effect glow

    pause 0.4

A: Second: what does the program cost? Mailings, slots, administration — all counted per name drawn, so the effect has to be counted per name drawn too.
    draw edge_q_budget branchlabel_q_budget node_budget label_budget

B: So two arrows already land on the diluted number.
    point at.ref node_budget gesture tap

A: Third, a different animal entirely: what does having Medicaid do to a person who has it? Four points divided by a take-up of a quarter — sixteen.
    draw edge_q_person branchlabel_q_person node_person label_person

A: That sixteen says nothing about Oregon's mailing list; it is a statement about coverage itself.
    focus node_person label_person branchlabel_q_person

B: Then a state running the same program should also find four points, right?
    point at.ref node_offer gesture circle

A: Only if the same quarter signs up. Double the take-up and the offer delivers twice as much, while the effect on a covered person has not budged.
    highlight label_offer label_person effect pulse delivery grave

A: So an agency deciding whether to fund outreach wants the intention-to-treat number, while an economist pricing a national expansion wants the rescaled one — quote the wrong one and you either oversell the program or undersell the insurance.
    focus node_offer node_person

A: Which is why the estimand is chosen up here, by the question — never down there, by the data.
    mark mark_q target label_q kind box

B: Same experiment, same regression — two questions, two different right answers.
    point at.ref mark_q gesture underline

A: Across this series: winning was not enrolling, comparing enrollees with non-enrollees threw the randomization away, and dividing by take-up bought it back — the diluted number was never damaged, it was simply the answer to a different question.
    delivery grave

